WEBVTT - Most Fascinating Stories - Episode 266: Charles Lindbergh: The Spirit of St. Louis, 33.5 Hours to Paris, and the Lone Eagle

1
00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:05.600
[Rebecca] Welcome to Most Fascinating Stories.

2
00:00:05.600 --> 00:00:06.880
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

3
00:00:06.880 --> 00:00:13.280
[Rebecca] Today, our top 50 famous non president American series chronicles the flight that shrank the globe.

4
00:00:13.280 --> 00:00:21.520
[Rebecca] A solitary 25-year-old airmail pilot who flew alone across 3600 miles of stormy ocean into immortality.

5
00:00:21.520 --> 00:00:27.200
[Rebecca] Here is George with the life of Charles Lindbergh.

6
00:00:27.200 --> 00:00:37.040
[George] Born in Detroit in February 1902 and raised on a farm along the Mississippi River in Little Falls, Minnesota, Charles Augustus Lindbergh J.R.

7
00:00:37.360 --> 00:00:44.000
[George] was the son of a fiery, antiwar populist United States congressman and a high school chemistry teacher.

8
00:00:44.000 --> 00:00:53.120
[George] From childhood, Slim was an introspective, mechanically gifted youth who loved tinkering with Model T engines and roaming the outdoors.

9
00:00:53.120 --> 00:01:08.880
[George] Dropping out of the University of Wisconsin's engineering program in 1922, Lindbergh enrolled in the Nebraska Aircraft Corporation's Flying School in Lincoln, learning to fly an antiquated World War I Curtis JN4 Jenny Biplane.

10
00:01:08.880 --> 00:01:19.280
[George] He spent two wild, perilous years as a barnstormer across the Midwest, performing Daredevil wing walking, parachute jumps, and stunt flying at county fairs.

11
00:01:19.280 --> 00:01:35.760
[George] In 1924, Lindbergh enlisted in the United States Army Air Service at Brooks Field in Texas, graduating top of his class as a pursuit pilot and surviving two midair collisions by parachuting to safety, earning entry into the Elite Caterpillar Club.

12
00:01:35.760 --> 00:01:57.360
[George] In 1926, Lindbergh became the chief airmail pilot for the Roberts Aircraft Corporation, flying the dangerous nighttime route between Sint Louie and Chicago in open cockpit to Havilland bipelanes through blinding blizzards and summer thunderstorms, twice bailing out into pitch black farm fields when running out of fuel in dense fog.

13
00:01:57.360 --> 00:02:08.520
[George] Young Charles' solitary excursions along the pine forested banks of the Mississippi River instilled in him an extraordinary self-reliance and emotional composure that never cracked under pressure.

14
00:02:08.520 --> 00:02:18.360
[George] His daredevil barn storming years across the prairie states taught him to read weather patterns from the cockpit and handle sudden engine failures over unfamiliar terrain.

15
00:02:18.360 --> 00:02:29.400
[George] In 1919, French American hotelier Raymond Ortegue offered a lavish $25,000 prize to the first aviator to fly non-stop between New York and Paris.

16
00:02:29.400 --> 00:02:33.480
[George] By late 1926, the challenge had turned deadly.

17
00:02:33.480 --> 00:02:40.440
[George] Famous World War I Aces and lavishly funded teams in multi-engine aircraft were crashing and dying in attempts.

18
00:02:40.440 --> 00:02:48.120
[George] Rene Fonc crashed on takeoff in a massive Sikorsky biplane, burning two crewmen alive, naval hero Richard E.

19
00:02:48.360 --> 00:02:59.000
[George] Bayad broke bones in trials, and French war heroes Charles Nungesser and Francois Colley vanished without a trace over the Atlantic in their biplane, the white bird.

20
00:02:59.000 --> 00:03:03.960
[George] Twenty-four-year-old Lindbergh approached the challenge with radical, minimalist logic.

21
00:03:03.960 --> 00:03:08.840
[George] Multi-engine planes were heavy, complex, and prone to engine failure.

22
00:03:08.840 --> 00:03:15.960
[George] A light, single-engine monoplane with a reliable air-cooled radial engine offered the highest probability of success.

23
00:03:15.960 --> 00:03:19.160
[George] Securing $15,000 from a syndicate of St.

24
00:03:19.160 --> 00:03:27.560
[George] Louis businessmen, Lindbergh hired Donald Hall and Ryan Airlines in San Diego to design the custom monoplane, named the Spirit of St.

25
00:03:27.560 --> 00:03:28.360
[George] Louis.

26
00:03:28.360 --> 00:03:31.400
[George] Lindbergh ruthlessly stripped every ounce of weight.

27
00:03:31.400 --> 00:03:33.240
[George] He carried no radio.

28
00:03:33.240 --> 00:03:48.080
[George] No sextant, no gas gauge, no parachute, and placed the massive main fuel tank directly in front of the cockpit to protect himself in a crash, completely blocking forward vision, requiring a small periscope to see directly ahead.

29
00:03:48.320 --> 00:03:57.520
[George] Donald Hall and the skilled craftsmen at Ryan Airlines worked in shifts around the clock in an abandoned fish cannery in San Diego to fabricate the spirit of St.

30
00:03:57.520 --> 00:03:59.520
[George] Louis in just 60 days.

31
00:03:59.520 --> 00:04:11.120
[George] Lindbergh's calculation that every pound of eliminated equipment allowed additional gallons of gasoline demonstrated an uncompromising engineering logic that made transatlantic flight possible.

32
00:04:11.120 --> 00:04:15.440
[George] On May 12, 1927, Lindbergh flew the spirit of St.

33
00:04:15.440 --> 00:04:22.160
[George] Louis from San Diego to New York in record time, arriving at Curtis and Roosevelt Fields on Long Island.

34
00:04:22.160 --> 00:04:26.800
[George] For a week, violent Atlantic rainstorms kept all aircraft grounded.

35
00:04:26.800 --> 00:04:39.840
[George] Unable to sleep due to excitement and journalists pounding on his hotel door, Lindbergh arrived at Roosevelt Field at dawn on Friday, May 20, 1927, having been awake for over 24 hours.

36
00:04:39.840 --> 00:04:43.680
[George] A cold gray drizzle fell across the muddy dirt runway.

37
00:04:43.680 --> 00:04:44.960
[George] The spirit of St.

38
00:04:45.200 --> 00:04:53.920
[George] Louis was loaded with 451 gallons of hyoctane gasoline, weighing over 5,000 pounds, double its empty weight.

39
00:04:53.920 --> 00:05:07.280
[George] Mechanics urged him to postpone, but Lindbergh climbed into his wicker seat, donned his leather flying helmet and goggles, and revved the 223 horsepower right whirlwind J5C radial engine.

40
00:05:07.280 --> 00:05:19.920
[George] At 7.52 a.m., the plane began its agonizingly slow crawl down the muddy runway, bouncing through puddles and barely clearing telephone wires and a tractor at the end of the field by 20 feet.

41
00:05:19.920 --> 00:05:25.360
[George] The lone eagle was airborne, heading out over the grey, white capped Atlantic Ocean.

42
00:05:25.360 --> 00:05:35.480
[George] The rain slicked runway at Roosevelt Field was surrounded by thousands of anxious spectators who held their breath as the overloaded monoplane struggled to lift into the morning mist.

43
00:05:35.720 --> 00:05:46.120
[George] Clearing the telephone wires at the runway's edge by barely twenty feet, Lindbergh banked toward the Long Island Sound, entering the vast solitude of the Atlantic Ocean.

44
00:05:46.120 --> 00:05:56.200
[George] The thirty-three and a half hour crossing of 3600 miles was an epic ordeal of human willpower against the overwhelming physical agony of sleep deprivation.

45
00:05:56.200 --> 00:06:08.360
[George] Flying by dead reckoning with only a magnetic compass, airspeed indicator, and an eight-day watch, Lindbergh skimmed ten feet above the foaming Atlantic waves to take advantage of ground effect.

46
00:06:08.360 --> 00:06:16.840
[George] Passing over Nova Scotia and Newfoundland, night fell as he entered towering walls of storm clouds and freezing fog over the Grand Banks.

47
00:06:16.840 --> 00:06:21.880
[George] Heavy sleet began coating the fabric wings, threatening to add fatal weight.

48
00:06:21.880 --> 00:06:27.080
[George] Lindbergh had to fly blind through freezing squalls, dodging ice-lads.

49
00:06:27.080 --> 00:06:31.480
[George] By the eighteenth hour, sheer exhaustion overwhelmed his nervous system.

50
00:06:31.480 --> 00:06:33.960
[George] His eyelids felt weighted with lead.

51
00:06:33.960 --> 00:06:45.320
[George] And he was forced to hold his eyes open with his fingers, leaning his face into the freezing gale through the open cockpit window and splashing cold water on his cheeks to prevent losing consciousness.

52
00:06:45.320 --> 00:06:51.400
[George] In the dark silence of the cockpit, Lindbergh experienced vivid, friendly hallucinations.

53
00:06:51.400 --> 00:06:59.880
[George] He saw ghostly, translucent phantoms floating through the cockpit, whispering words of encouragement and guiding his navigational headings.

54
00:06:59.880 --> 00:07:10.840
[George] At dawn, skimming over fishing fleets off the coast of Ireland, Lindbergh circled a fishing smack, throttling down his engine and shouting out the window Which way is Ireland?

55
00:07:10.840 --> 00:07:13.960
[George] The stunned fisherman stood paralyzed in silence.

56
00:07:13.960 --> 00:07:25.840
[George] Flying through towering storm walls and blinding sleet over the Grand Banks, Lindbergh relied entirely on his compass and airspeed indicator, fighting the physical delirium of sleep deprivation.

57
00:07:25.840 --> 00:07:37.280
[George] The friendly, translucent phantoms that populated his cockpit during the long night hours spoke to the profound psychological strain of solitary endurance at the frontier of human possibility.

58
00:07:37.280 --> 00:07:50.240
[George] Crossing the green coastal headlands of southwestern Ireland at Dingle Bay, Lindbergh discovered to his astonishment that his dead reckoning navigation was barely three miles off course after two thousand miles of ocean flight.

59
00:07:50.240 --> 00:07:58.080
[George] Crossing the English Channel at sunset, he followed the gleaming silver ribbon of the Seine River toward the radiant glow of Paris.

60
00:07:58.080 --> 00:07:59.760
[George] At 1022 p.m.

61
00:07:59.760 --> 00:08:15.280
[George] local time on Saturday, May 21, 1927, 33 hours, 30 minutes and 29 seconds after leaving New York, the silver monoplane touched down gracefully on the grass runways of La Burgut Aerodrome outside Paris.

62
00:08:15.280 --> 00:08:18.480
[George] Lindbergh expected a few curious mechanics.

63
00:08:18.480 --> 00:08:30.000
[George] Instead, an overwhelming tidal wave of 150,000 hysterical spectators broke through military police lines and surged toward the airplane like an unstoppable tsunami.

64
00:08:30.000 --> 00:08:44.400
[George] Spectators hoisted Lindbergh onto their shoulders, cheering, weeping, and tearing fabric souvenirs from the wings, while two French aviators cleverly whisked Lindbergh away into an unmarked car to save him from being crushed to death by love.

65
00:08:44.400 --> 00:08:50.560
[George] At twenty five years old, Charles Lindbergh had become the most famous human being on planet Earth.

66
00:08:50.560 --> 00:09:01.560
[George] The joyous roar of the 150,000 spectators at La Burgett Field broke all police barricades, celebrating the triumph of an unassuming youth who had shrunk the globe.

67
00:09:01.560 --> 00:09:12.520
[George] The French government awarded Lindbergh the Legion of Honor, while crowds outside his Paris hotel chanted his name through the night in an unprecedented outpouring of international affection.

68
00:09:12.520 --> 00:09:24.840
[George] Returning to America aboard the cruiser USS Memphis, Lindbergh was awarded the Medal of Honor, and over four million cheering citizens showered him with 1800 tons of ticker tape in New York City.

69
00:09:24.840 --> 00:09:30.200
[George] In 1929, Lindbergh married Anne Morrow, the gifted daughter of the U.S.

70
00:09:30.520 --> 00:09:41.320
[George] ambassador to Mexico, pioneering global commercial airline routes together for Pan American World Airways, but global fame exacted a horrific, tragic price.

71
00:09:41.320 --> 00:09:58.760
[George] On the stormy night of March 1, 1932, their 20-month-old first-born son, Charles Augustus Lindbergh J.R., was kidnapped from his second-floor nursery crib in their secluded home near Hopewell, New Jersey, leaving behind a crude ransom note on the radiator.

72
00:09:58.760 --> 00:10:03.720
[George] For ten agonizing weeks, the entire nation followed the frantic search.

73
00:10:03.720 --> 00:10:17.960
[George] Despite paying a $50,000 cash ransom in a Bronx cemetery, the child's battered, decomposed body was found dumped in a shallow roadside ditch two months later, having suffered a fractured skull on the night of the abduction.

74
00:10:17.960 --> 00:10:31.080
[George] The crime of the century and the sensational 1935 trial and execution of German immigrant carpenter Bruno Richard Hauptmann drove the shattered, hunted Lindbergh family into bitter self imposed exile in England.

75
00:10:31.080 --> 00:10:41.640
[George] The ticker tape parade in New York City showered Lindbergh with over 1800 tons of paper, a rapturous welcome that overwhelmed the shy, modest Midwestern aviator.

76
00:10:41.640 --> 00:10:44.840
[George] The horrifying abduction and murder of baby Charles J.R.

77
00:10:45.440 --> 00:10:53.280
[George] destroyed the family's peace, exposing the toxic price of uncontrolled sensationalist media celebrity in modern America.

78
00:10:53.280 --> 00:10:58.640
[George] In the late 1930s, Lindbergh's reputation suffered a catastrophic eclipse.

79
00:10:58.640 --> 00:11:10.320
[George] Touring German aircraft factories at the request of the American military, Lindbergh was awarded a civilian medal by Hermann Göring and became the charismatic public leader of the America First Committee.

80
00:11:10.320 --> 00:11:20.800
[George] Arguing passionately against American intervention in World War II and delivering controversial speeches that drew fierce accusations of antisemitism and Nazi appeasement.

81
00:11:20.800 --> 00:11:26.080
[George] Following the attack on Pearl Harbor, Lindbergh immediately volunteered for service.

82
00:11:26.080 --> 00:11:42.640
[George] Though President Roosevelt refused to restore his military commission, Lindbergh traveled to the Pacific Theater as a civilian technical consultant for United Aircraft, flying 50 unauthorized combat combat missions in P-38 Lightnings and F-4U Corsairs.

83
00:11:42.640 --> 00:11:49.600
[George] Teaching pilots fuel conservation techniques and shooting down a Japanese reconnaissance plane in aerial combat.

84
00:11:49.600 --> 00:12:00.000
[George] In his later years, Lindbergh became a passionate conservationist, living on the remote coast of Maui, Hawaii, where he died of lymphoma in August 1974.

85
00:12:00.000 --> 00:12:05.600
[George] Charles Lindbergh's life embodied the dizzying heights and tragic depths of the 20th century.

86
00:12:05.600 --> 00:12:13.840
[George] The quiet Midwestern youth whose courage, precision, and 33 hours of solitary grace opened the skies and united a planet.

87
00:12:13.840 --> 00:12:24.720
[George] Lindbergh's unauthorized combat missions in the Pacific Theater showcased his peerless aeronautical intuition, extending the combat range of American fighters by hundreds of miles.

88
00:12:24.720 --> 00:12:35.400
[George] His final years of ecological advocacy in the Pacific reflected his mature realization that technological advancement must be balanced by a deep reverence for the natural world.

89
00:12:46.680 --> 00:12:53.160
[Rebecca] Amelia Earhart, The Queen of the Air, The Transatlantic Solo, and The Mystery of Howland Island.

90
00:12:53.160 --> 00:12:55.720
[Rebecca] Until then, stay curious.

